# Pin LF for the frozen governance mapping so its bytes — and therefore the
# SHA-256 pinned in tests/regression/test_document_governance.py, docs/baselines.md
# and docs/plans/m7-p0-corpus-governance-report.md — are identical on every
# platform. Without this, core.autocrlf produces CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux.
docs/reference/document-mapping.json text eol=lf
# M8 gate records and experiment identities are canonical JSON (sa-json-c14n-v1:
# UTF-8 without BOM, LF terminated). Their digests are pinned inside sibling records,
# so a CRLF checkout changes the bytes a verifier reads and breaks the chain on
# Windows only. Pinning LF keeps on-disk bytes equal to committed bytes everywhere.
docs/plans/references/external-gates/**/*.json text eol=lf
docs/plans/references/external-artifacts/**/*.json text eol=lf
# The active protocol blob is the digest root of the whole gate chain: every
# draft-0.9 record pins its SHA-256, so a CRLF checkout would make those records
# stop verifying on any machine whose line endings differ. Pinned per file
# rather than by wildcard, deliberately: draft-0.5 is frozen history whose
# records pin the pre-pin bytes, and rewriting it is out of scope (decision D1a
# of m8-active-execution-protocol-draft-0.10-authorization-20260913.md).
docs/plans/references/m8-active-execution-protocol-draft-0.9.md text eol=lf
docs/plans/references/m8-active-execution-protocol-draft-0.10.md text eol=lf

# draft-0.11 successor protocol: A+B+C P3-blocker revision authorized by
# m8-active-execution-protocol-draft-0.11-authorization-20260914.md.
docs/plans/references/m8-active-execution-protocol-draft-0.11.md text eol=lf

